{"context":"- Anvil is newly released; most users and most AI coding agents do not know what Anvil is, how it works, or its best practices. The gap blocks agents from being effective inside Anvil projects.\n- The proposal: bundle AI agent \"skills\" (structured guidance agents load on demand) directly into binaries, add a CLI command surface to install them, and cover two sources — core (the Anvil CLI itself) and the standards (flutter, laravel — the adapter/standard binaries). The install gate stays in the core binary; when a standard is installed, its skills become registered with core, detectable and installable.\n- The AI agent ecosystem has converged on one open standard — Agent Skills (agentskills.io): a `\u003cname\u003e/SKILL.md` folder with YAML frontmatter (`name`, `description`), read natively by Claude  Source: ADR-037-ai-agent-skills-distribution.md Date:2026-08-12 Status:Proposed.","decision":"**Skills are a governed distribution surface, not a new distribution channel.**\n\n- **D1 — Format: Agent Skills spec (agentskills.io).** Every Anvil skill is a `\u003cname\u003e/SKILL.md` folder with YAML frontmatter restricted to the portable spec fields (`name`, `description`, `license`, `compatibility`, `metadata`, `allowed-tools`). Agent-specific frontmatter fields (e.g. Claude Code `context: fork`) are forbidden in Anvil-distributed skills so one artifact works across agents.\n- **D2 — Core skills are embedded; standard skills are release assets.** Core skills ship inside the Anvil binary via `go:embed` (lockstep with the CLI version they describe — a separable update cycle would drift). Standard skills are packed per-skill assets (`anvil-skill-\u003cname\u003e-\u003cversion\u003e.tar.gz`) in the standard's release ","consequences":"- **Positive:** agents gain up-to-date Anvil knowledge from the exact binary/standard they use; skills are verifiable, updateable, uninstallable, and portable across agents; no new distribution channel or trust machinery; core and standard skills follow the same install gate.\n- **Negative:** the registry metadata schema is extended (`skills[]` + named skill assets) — a governed artifact (ADR-029 §2.4); the schema change requires a forward-compatibility decision for older CLI parsers (tolerate additive optional fields within a deprecation window, ADR-024 spirit) before implementation. Command surface grows by one group (`skill`); documented in the command-implementation checklist. Standard publishers must author and release skill assets as part of their release pipeline.","alternativesConsidered":"Considered\n\n- **Separate skill registry/repo (skills marketplace).** Rejected: a parallel distribution channel violates ADR-025 (post-split) and ADR-030 (https-only content); the standard's own release channel already carries trusted, attested assets.\n- **Register skills by querying the adapter binary** (`anvil-adapter-\u003cname\u003e --list-skills`). Rejected: executes not-yet-trusted code just to learn its contents; the adapter may not be installed; adds a governed contract change; the adapter carries "}